.TH QRQ 1 "August 2026" Linux "User Manuals"
.SH NAME
qrq \- High speed Morse telegraphy trainer

.SH SYNOPSIS
.B qrq 

.B qrqscore
[OPTION]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B qrq
is an open source Morse code 
trainer for various platforms and operating
systems, similar to the classic DOS
version of Rufz by DL4MM.

It's not intended for learning telegraphy (have a look at
http://radio.linux.org.au/ for CW learning software), but to improve the
ability to copy callsigns at high speeds, as needed for example for contesting. 

After starting the software and setting up the parameters, the program sends
50 callsigns or words by default. When a callsign is copied correctly, the
speed increases by the configured up step; otherwise it decreases by the
configured down step. A character-speed floor enables Farnsworth spacing when
the overall speed falls below it. Points depend on speed and copy accuracy.

F5 opens three settings pages; Tab selects general, audio, or call-filter
settings. F6 repeats the current item. During an attempt F7 replays the
previous item; outside an attempt it shows local session history. F10 aborts
the attempt or exits the program.

Only completed, ordinary 50-call sessions are added to the comparable local
toplist. Fixed-speed, unlimited-repeat, non-50-call, adaptive, missed-item
review, and seeded sessions remain in history but are toplist-ineligible.
Aborted sessions are excluded, and an optional accuracy goal must be met.

A scoreboard/toplist exists on the author's website, at
https://fkurz.net/ham/qrqtop.php. The local toplist file can be synchronized
with the online toplist with 
.B qrqscore -d
and you can upload your own top scores by invoking
.B qrqscore -u.

.SH FILES
.I qrqrc
.RS
Default configuration file
.RE
.I callbase.qcb
.RS
Database of amateur radio callsigns used by qrq. Other files may be loaded.
.RE
.I toplist
.RS
Default toplist file
.RE
.I toplist.history.csv
.RS
Versioned local session history, including training sessions
.RE
.I Summary/
.RS
Detailed, uniquely named summaries of attempts
.RE

.B qrq
is looking for these files in the current directory, in ~/.qrq/ or under
PREFIX/share/qrq/ (in that order). PREFIX defaults to /usr and is selected at
build time. If installed resources are used, ~/.qrq/ is created for the user's
toplist, history, summaries, and configuration.
.SH AUTHOR
Fabian Kurz, DJ5CW <fabian@fkurz.net> - https://fkurz.net/ham/qrq.html
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR morse (1)
